1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,325
Welcome to Session nine.

2
00:00:02,325 --> 00:00:06,350
Three sessions form one arc, and this is the last of them.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:14,225
Session seven asked what a network can represent: one weighted sum draws one straight boundary, and a hidden layer draws more.

4
00:00:14,225 --> 00:00:21,650
Session eight asked how we would know whether it generalises: three data sets, honest metrics, and a split that does not leak.

5
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:29,900
Today asks the question we have been putting off since Session seven: how do you actually train one, and why does training so often not work?

6
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,375
And I want to be honest about the balance of this session.

7
00:00:33,375 --> 00:00:36,525
Maybe a quarter of it is how training works.

8
00:00:36,525 --> 00:00:41,375
The rest is how training fails, which is where your time will actually go.

9
00:00:41,375 --> 00:00:44,875
Everybody can call dot backward and dot step.

10
00:00:44,875 --> 00:01:03,150
The skill that distinguishes somebody who can use these tools from somebody who cannot is recognising the shape of a failure: knowing, when a loss curve sits flat, whether the problem is your initialisation, your activation function, your learning rate, your data, or the fact that you are asking for something impossible.

11
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:07,525
We use four datasets today, deliberately different from each other.

12
00:01:07,525 --> 00:01:11,150
Breast-cancer biopsies for the training loop itself.

13
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:18,675
A protein fitness landscape called GB1 for the failures, and for seeing when a hidden layer actually pays for itself.

14
00:01:18,675 --> 00:01:22,375
Session eight's localisation data for overfitting.

15
00:01:22,375 --> 00:01:25,225
And blood-cell images in your lab.

16
00:01:25,225 --> 00:01:26,850
Three blocks.

17
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:29,850
From an untrained network to a trained one.

18
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:32,025
Why training fails.

19
00:01:32,025 --> 00:01:36,700
And overfitting, with the question of whether an improvement you see is real.

20
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:42,675
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session nine page of the course book.

21
00:01:42,675 --> 00:01:50,900
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

22
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:54,925
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

23
00:01:55,525 --> 00:01:58,950
Let us pick up exactly where Session eight stopped.

24
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:07,400
At the end of that session we built the localisation network, pushed one batch through it to check the shapes, and evaluated it with random weights.

25
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:15,275
Accuracy nought point one eight one, MCC minus nought point zero two seven, AUROC nought point four seven four.

26
00:02:15,275 --> 00:02:17,375
All three are chance.

27
00:02:17,375 --> 00:02:24,200
That is the honest starting point of every network: a pile of random numbers that computes something arbitrary.

28
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:29,100
Everything that makes it useful is the training procedure, and that is today.

29
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:39,675
The picture on this slide is the arc of the three sessions, and I put it up because the three questions really are separate, and confusing them causes most of the trouble I see in projects.

30
00:02:39,675 --> 00:02:42,850
What can this model represent, in principle?

31
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:45,425
Would I know if it generalised?

32
00:02:45,425 --> 00:02:48,375
And is my training actually working?

33
00:02:48,375 --> 00:02:52,125
A model can be perfectly capable and badly trained.

34
00:02:52,125 --> 00:02:55,875
It can be well trained and useless because it has memorised.

35
00:02:55,875 --> 00:03:00,700
It can be capable and well trained and still be evaluated dishonestly.

36
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:04,950
Three questions, three sessions, and you need all three answers.

37
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:07,025
Block one.

38
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:10,025
From an untrained network to a trained one.

39
00:03:10,025 --> 00:03:19,400
On the page, this block is the sections "From an untrained network to a trained network", "How backpropagation finds every gradient" and "Which loss to minimise".

40
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:25,025
This block is the mechanics, and it is the shortest and least treacherous part of the session.

41
00:03:25,025 --> 00:03:36,325
Five lines of code that run over and over; what backpropagation computes and why it is cheap; mini-batches; and the loss function, where there is one choice that matters more than people expect.

42
00:03:36,925 --> 00:03:42,500
First the data, because I would rather teach the loop on something real than on random numbers.

43
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:46,175
Five hundred and sixty-nine fine-needle biopsies.

44
00:03:46,175 --> 00:03:52,375
A fine-needle aspiration takes a small sample of cells from a breast lump with a thin needle.

45
00:03:52,375 --> 00:04:02,425
The cells are photographed under a microscope, and software measures properties of the cell nuclei: radius, texture, perimeter, smoothness, concavity, and so on.

46
00:04:02,425 --> 00:04:04,950
Thirty numbers per biopsy.

47
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:08,775
The label is whether that lump was benign or malignant.

48
00:04:08,775 --> 00:04:21,525
So it is a two-class classification problem with thirty features, and it is a real clinical question, although I should say clearly that this is a teaching dataset from the nineteen nineties and not a diagnostic tool.

49
00:04:21,525 --> 00:04:27,050
Our network: thirty inputs, a hidden layer of sixteen ReLU units, one output.

50
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:30,375
Five hundred and thirteen weights altogether.

51
00:04:30,375 --> 00:04:33,950
Small, by design, so that everything is visible.

52
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:37,350
One detail that is easy to skip and matters.

53
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:46,850
The features are standardised, meaning each is shifted and scaled to have mean zero and standard deviation one, because they arrive in wildly different units.

54
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:52,325
And the mean and standard deviation used for that are computed from the training set only.

55
00:04:52,325 --> 00:04:59,200
If you compute them from all the data, information about your test set has entered your model before you started.

56
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:05,600
That is Session eight's preprocessing leak, and it is one of the most common real mistakes in published work.

57
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:08,475
Here is the training loop.

58
00:05:08,475 --> 00:05:19,150
It is five lines, and it is the same five lines in essentially every PyTorch program ever written, from this one to the ones that train models with billions of parameters.

59
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:22,925
Line one: optimiser dot zero underscore grad.

60
00:05:22,925 --> 00:05:26,225
This clears the gradients from the previous step.

61
00:05:26,225 --> 00:05:28,575
Why does that need saying?

62
00:05:28,575 --> 00:05:35,375
Because PyTorch accumulates gradients by default: it adds new gradients to whatever is already stored.

63
00:05:35,375 --> 00:05:46,425
That is deliberate and occasionally useful, and if you forget this line your updates are computed from the sum of every batch so far, and your training quietly does something incoherent.

64
00:05:46,425 --> 00:05:48,950
Line two: the forward pass.

65
00:05:48,950 --> 00:05:54,925
Push the batch through the model and get the outputs, which, as Session eight insisted, are raw logits.

66
00:05:54,925 --> 00:05:57,450
Line three: the criterion.

67
00:05:57,450 --> 00:06:01,975
Compare those outputs with the true labels and get one number, the loss.

68
00:06:01,975 --> 00:06:04,600
Line four: loss dot backward.

69
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,900
This is backpropagation.

70
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:12,075
It computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every single weight in the model.

71
00:06:12,075 --> 00:06:15,350
We will look at what it does on the next slide but one.

72
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:18,375
Line five: optimiser dot step.

73
00:06:18,375 --> 00:06:22,350
Take each weight and move it a little way against its gradient.

74
00:06:22,350 --> 00:06:26,775
Session seven's update rule, applied to all the weights at once.

75
00:06:26,775 --> 00:06:33,000
And then you do that again, for the next batch, and the next, until the data runs out, which is one epoch.

76
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,525
And then you do the epochs again.

77
00:06:35,525 --> 00:06:37,200
That is it.

78
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:39,200
There is nothing else.

79
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:45,425
In the lab you write this loop yourself, once, so that every framework you meet later is recognisable.

80
00:06:46,025 --> 00:06:49,525
And here is what those five lines do when you run them.

81
00:06:49,525 --> 00:06:54,200
On the left, the loss per epoch, two curves: training and validation.

82
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:56,700
Both fall steeply at first.

83
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:04,675
The validation loss reaches its lowest point at epoch fifty-two and then stops improving, while the training loss keeps going down.

84
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:09,000
Remember that divergence; it is Block three's whole subject.

85
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,800
On the right, the predictions themselves, before and after.

86
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:17,425
Before training, every single prediction sits near nought point five.

87
00:07:17,425 --> 00:07:20,350
The network has no idea and says so.

88
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:26,475
The numbers: validation loss nought point six five one, accuracy nought point six five nine.

89
00:07:26,475 --> 00:07:29,325
And notice what that accuracy is.

90
00:07:29,325 --> 00:07:33,500
It is roughly the proportion of benign biopsies in the data.

91
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:44,625
The network is effectively answering benign every time, which is Session eight's point about accuracy on unbalanced data, appearing here as the natural behaviour of an untrained model.

92
00:07:44,625 --> 00:07:53,350
After training: the benign cases have moved down towards zero and the malignant ones up towards one, with a clear gap between the two clouds.

93
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:58,800
Validation loss nought point one zero nine, accuracy nought point nine five three.

94
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:10,425
That is the whole of machine learning in one picture: a pile of random numbers became a function that separates two clinical categories, by doing five lines of arithmetic a few thousand times.

95
00:08:11,025 --> 00:08:15,575
And then, as Session eight insisted, we look at the test set once.

96
00:08:15,575 --> 00:08:18,450
Accuracy nought point nine five three.

97
00:08:18,450 --> 00:08:21,600
MCC nought point nine zero zero.

98
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:24,525
AUROC nought point nine eight four.

99
00:08:24,525 --> 00:08:27,775
Eighty-six test biopsies, four mistakes.

100
00:08:27,775 --> 00:08:30,625
Two false alarms and two missed.

101
00:08:30,625 --> 00:08:36,725
Let me dwell on those four, because the split matters clinically and it is a good habit to think this way.

102
00:08:36,725 --> 00:08:45,475
Two false alarms means two benign lumps were called malignant: those patients get a frightening phone call and a further test that comes back clear.

103
00:08:45,475 --> 00:08:50,875
Two missed means two malignant lumps were called benign: those patients are sent home.

104
00:08:50,875 --> 00:08:58,450
Those two errors are not interchangeable, and which one you would rather make is a question for a clinician, not for a statistician.

105
00:08:58,450 --> 00:09:10,800
It is the precision-versus-recall decision from Session eight, and in a real deployment you would move the threshold away from nought point five deliberately to make the misses rarer, accepting more false alarms.

106
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,425
The model gives you a probability.

107
00:09:13,425 --> 00:09:16,350
Where you cut it is a value judgement.

108
00:09:16,950 --> 00:09:24,150
Now a question I want you to be in the habit of asking about your own models, and the answer here is a good lesson in modesty.

109
00:09:24,150 --> 00:09:26,525
Was the hidden layer needed?

110
00:09:26,525 --> 00:09:34,650
Take the same data and fit plain logistic regression: one weighted sum, thirty-one weights instead of five hundred and thirteen.

111
00:09:34,650 --> 00:09:36,525
Same accuracy.

112
00:09:36,525 --> 00:09:40,650
The network, with sixteen times the parameters, bought nothing.

113
00:09:40,650 --> 00:09:41,475
Why?

114
00:09:41,475 --> 00:09:44,500
Because this data is nearly additive.

115
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:52,950
Malignant and benign nuclei differ mainly in being bigger and more irregular, and those thirty measurements mostly just point the same way.

116
00:09:52,950 --> 00:09:57,975
A weighted sum of them separates the classes about as well as anything can.

117
00:09:57,975 --> 00:10:04,550
There is no interaction between features for a hidden layer to discover, so the hidden layer has nothing to do.

118
00:10:04,550 --> 00:10:07,800
One methodological note on how we checked.

119
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:17,425
We used cross-validation for this comparison, not a single split, because eighty-six test biopsies is far too few to distinguish two models that are close.

120
00:10:17,425 --> 00:10:22,175
That is Session eight's lesson about split-to-split variability, applied.

121
00:10:22,175 --> 00:10:25,300
And do not read this as a general result.

122
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:32,725
Block two uses a dataset where the hidden layer emphatically does pay off, and the comparison between the two is the point.

123
00:10:33,325 --> 00:10:39,700
Now the question at the heart of the session: when the loss is too high, which weights should change, and by how much?

124
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:46,475
The answer is the chain rule, which you met in Session one, applied systematically from the loss backwards.

125
00:10:46,475 --> 00:10:49,200
That is what backpropagation is.

126
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:55,800
It is not a separate algorithm with its own ideas; it is the chain rule plus one piece of bookkeeping.

127
00:10:55,800 --> 00:11:05,275
Take a tiny network: an input goes through a weight to a hidden unit, the hidden unit goes through another weight to the output, and the output goes into the loss.

128
00:11:05,275 --> 00:11:17,550
To know how the loss responds to the first weight, you multiply three things: how the loss responds to the output, how the output responds to the hidden unit, and how the hidden unit responds to that weight.

129
00:11:17,550 --> 00:11:20,275
Three derivatives, multiplied.

130
00:11:20,275 --> 00:11:24,900
Now here is the bookkeeping, which is the thing that makes it practical.

131
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:31,200
That first factor, how the loss responds to the output, is needed for every weight in the network.

132
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:34,025
So you compute it once and keep it.

133
00:11:34,025 --> 00:11:42,350
Then you multiply by the next factor to get the error signal at the hidden layer, and that is shared by every weight feeding into that hidden unit.

134
00:11:42,350 --> 00:11:45,325
And so on, backwards, layer by layer.

135
00:11:45,325 --> 00:11:55,900
Because every layer's error signal is computed once and reused for all of its weights, getting the gradient of every weight in the network costs about as much as one forward pass.

136
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:59,550
Not one pass per weight: one pass in total.

137
00:11:59,550 --> 00:12:03,100
That is the whole reason deep learning is possible.

138
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:12,650
If you had to compute each weight's gradient separately, a model with a million weights would need a million passes per step, and nobody would ever have trained anything.

139
00:12:13,250 --> 00:12:16,975
And here is the demonstration that it is not magic.

140
00:12:16,975 --> 00:12:22,225
The notebook takes a tiny network with five weights, and differentiates it by hand.

141
00:12:22,225 --> 00:12:28,100
Writes out the chain rule for each weight, and evaluates it, with arithmetic you could do on paper.

142
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:33,675
Then it calls loss dot backward on the same network and prints what PyTorch computed.

143
00:12:33,675 --> 00:12:36,550
They agree to eight decimal places.

144
00:12:36,550 --> 00:12:43,000
I think it is worth doing this once in your life, and the notebook does it so that you can see it without the work.

145
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:55,850
After that, you use autograd and stop thinking about it, which is correct: the library is not approximating anything, it is applying the same chain rule you would, with perfect bookkeeping and no arithmetic slips.

146
00:12:55,850 --> 00:13:00,600
What it does not do is save you from understanding what the gradient means.

147
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:07,175
Block two is entirely about situations where the gradient is computed perfectly and is useless.

148
00:13:07,775 --> 00:13:11,000
One practical piece before the loss function.

149
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,675
You do not compute the gradient on all the data at once.

150
00:13:14,675 --> 00:13:22,150
You shuffle the training set, cut it into batches of, say, thirty-two biopsies, and update the weights after every batch.

151
00:13:22,150 --> 00:13:27,375
This is stochastic gradient descent, which you heard named in Session seven.

152
00:13:27,375 --> 00:13:28,200
Why?

153
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:30,975
Trading accuracy for frequency.

154
00:13:30,975 --> 00:13:37,300
The gradient from thirty-two examples is a noisy estimate of the true gradient over all the data.

155
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:46,250
But you get it for a thirty-second of the cost, so in the time one exact step would take, you take many approximate ones, and you get much further downhill.

156
00:13:46,250 --> 00:13:54,700
The noise turns out to be mildly helpful too, since it can shake the model out of poor solutions, though that argument is easy to over-tell.

157
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:57,000
Two practical details.

158
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Shuffle every epoch, not once, so the model never sees the same sequence of batches twice.

159
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,350
In PyTorch a DataLoader does this for you.

160
00:14:06,350 --> 00:14:12,625
And measure the validation loss after every epoch, keeping the weights from whichever epoch was best.

161
00:14:12,625 --> 00:14:19,400
That is early stopping, which is already a regularisation method, and we will come back to it in Block three.

162
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:23,450
For our breast-cancer network, the best epoch was fifty-two.

163
00:14:24,050 --> 00:14:29,375
Now the loss function, where there is one decision that matters more than people expect.

164
00:14:29,375 --> 00:14:32,475
For classification, use cross-entropy.

165
00:14:32,475 --> 00:14:38,525
The formula is simple: minus the logarithm of the probability your model gave to the true class.

166
00:14:38,525 --> 00:14:41,925
Put numbers in, and the shape of it becomes clear.

167
00:14:41,925 --> 00:14:49,825
If the model gives the true class a probability of nought point nine, the cost is nought point one zero five: nearly nothing.

168
00:14:49,825 --> 00:14:56,800
If it gives the true class nought point zero one, meaning it was confidently wrong, the cost is four point six.

169
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:59,200
Forty times the penalty.

170
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:05,750
And it keeps going: as the probability of the true class approaches zero, the cost goes to infinity.

171
00:15:05,750 --> 00:15:09,600
So cross-entropy is not just measuring whether you were wrong.

172
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:16,075
It is measuring how confidently you were wrong, and punishing confident mistakes very harshly indeed.

173
00:15:16,075 --> 00:15:18,075
That is a feature.

174
00:15:18,075 --> 00:15:22,700
It is what pushes a model towards being honest about its uncertainty.

175
00:15:22,700 --> 00:15:25,925
And it is Session one's material, returning.

176
00:15:25,925 --> 00:15:31,975
Cross-entropy is surprise: the surprise of seeing the true answer, given what the model believed.

177
00:15:31,975 --> 00:15:40,850
Averaged over a dataset, it is the negative log-likelihood, so minimising cross-entropy is exactly maximum likelihood estimation.

178
00:15:40,850 --> 00:15:46,375
If you liked Session one's statistics, that connection is worth a minute of your own time.

179
00:15:46,375 --> 00:15:52,550
For regression, use the mean squared error instead, for the same reason you did in Session seven.

180
00:15:52,550 --> 00:16:06,700
And the PyTorch note from Session eight, repeated because it still catches people: BCEWithLogitsLoss for two classes and CrossEntropyLoss for more combine the sigmoid or log-softmax with the loss in one numerically stable step.

181
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:09,400
Your model outputs raw logits.

182
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:12,375
Do not apply the activation yourself.

183
00:16:12,975 --> 00:16:18,625
And here is what happens when you get that choice wrong, which the lab makes you see for yourself.

184
00:16:18,625 --> 00:16:22,650
Same network, same data, eight classes of blood cell.

185
00:16:22,650 --> 00:16:25,450
Only the loss function changes.

186
00:16:25,450 --> 00:16:31,800
With mean squared error, after twenty epochs, the network predicts only five of the eight classes.

187
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:34,750
With mean absolute error, only four.

188
00:16:34,750 --> 00:16:40,250
Three or four classes have simply been abandoned: the network never predicts them at all.

189
00:16:40,250 --> 00:16:43,200
With cross-entropy, it uses all eight.

190
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:44,025
Why?

191
00:16:44,025 --> 00:16:49,475
Think about what mean squared error does to a confident mistake in a classification problem.

192
00:16:49,475 --> 00:16:57,450
The outputs are bounded, so the worst possible error is bounded too, and squaring a number smaller than one makes it smaller still.

193
00:16:57,450 --> 00:17:00,100
A confident mistake is cheap.

194
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:10,225
So the gradient pushing the classes apart is weak, and the easiest way to reduce the loss is to hedge towards the common classes and quietly give up on the rare ones.

195
00:17:10,225 --> 00:17:14,450
Cross-entropy's unbounded penalty is what prevents that.

196
00:17:14,450 --> 00:17:21,025
The model cannot afford to be confidently wrong about a rare class, so it has to keep that class alive.

197
00:17:21,025 --> 00:17:24,825
The practical rule: classification, cross-entropy.

198
00:17:24,825 --> 00:17:27,175
Regression, squared error.

199
00:17:27,175 --> 00:17:29,725
It is not a matter of taste.

